  "answer_text": "There are **three common ways** to change a `<span>`'s color on mouse over:\n\n---\n\n## \u2705 Method 1: Using CSS `:hover` (Recommended)\n\nThis is the simplest and most recommended approach. You can create a hover effect using the `:hover` pseudo-class selector and CSS.\n\n```html\n<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html>\n  <head>\n    <style>\n      span {\n        color: black;\n        cursor: pointer;\n      }\n\n      span:hover {\n        color: red;\n      }\n    </style>\n  </head>\n  <body>\n    <span>Hover over me!</span>\n  </body>\n</html>\n```\n\n> When the mouse hovers over the `<span>`, the text color changes to red.\n\n---\n\n## \u2705 Method 2: Using a CSS Class with `:hover`\n\nIf you have multiple `<span>` elements but only want specific ones to change color, assign a class to the target spans.\n\n```html\n<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html>\n  <head>\n    <style>\n      .highlight-span {\n        color: black;\n        cursor: pointer;\n      }\n\n      .highlight-span:hover {\n        color: blue;\n      }\n    </style>\n  </head>\n  <body>\n    <span class=\"highlight-span\">Hover over me!</span>\n    <span>I won't change color.</span>\n  </body>\n</html>\n```\n\nYou can name your classes anything you want. Whatever name you choose, just make sure the name used in your HTML matches the one in your CSS rules.\n\n---\n\n## \u2705 Method 3: Using JavaScript `onmouseover` / `onmouseout`\n\nYou can also do it with JavaScript inline events:\n\n```html\n<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html>\n  <body>\n    <span\n      onmouseover=\"this.style.color='red';\"\n      onmouseout=\"this.style.color='black';\"\n    >\n      Hover over me!\n    </span>\n  </body>\n</html>\n```\n\n> `onmouseover` triggers when the mouse enters the element, and `onmouseout` reverts the color when the mouse leaves.\n\n---\n\n## \ud83c\udfc6 Which Method Should You Use?\n\n| Method | Pros | Cons |\n|---|---|---|\n| **CSS `:hover`** | Clean, simple, no JS needed | None for basic use |\n| **CSS Class + `:hover`** | Targets specific elements | Requires adding a class |\n| **JavaScript** | Dynamic control | Mixes style with logic |\n\nThe **CSS `:hover` method** is strongly preferred as it keeps styling in CSS where it belongs, is easier to maintain, and works without JavaScript.",
